{"question": "A 43-year-old man comes to the emergency department with nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and progressive perioral numbness for the past 24 hours. 3 days ago, he underwent a total thyroidectomy for treatment of papillary thyroid cancer. His only medication is a multivitamin supplement. He appears fatigued. While measuring the patient's blood pressure, the nurse observes a spasm in the patient's hand. Physical examination shows a well-healing surgical wound on the neck. Which of the following ECG findings are most likely in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Torsade de pointes", "B": "QT prolongation", "C": "U waves", "D": "Peaked T waves", "E": "PR prolongation"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Endocrinology", "Cardiology", "Surgery", "Gastroenterology"], "option_domains": ["Cardiology", "Endocrinology"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "Based on the medical scenario provided, the patient is a 43-year-old man who has recently undergone a total thyroidectomy for papillary thyroid cancer and is now presenting with symptoms of nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and progressive perioral numbness. The patient also exhibits a spasm in his hand while his blood pressure is being measured. The physical examination reveals a well-healing surgical wound on the neck.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition\n\nThe symptoms and physical findings suggest a possible complication related to the thyroidectomy, specifically hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia can occur post-thyroidectomy, especially if the parathyroid glands were damaged during the surgery. Parathyroid glands are responsible for regulating calcium levels in the body. When they are damaged, it can lead to a decrease in parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels, resulting in hypocalcemia.\n\n### Key Aspects of the Issue\n\n1. **Progressive Perioral Numbness**: This is a classic symptom of hypocalcemia. It is often the first sign of low calcium levels